{"connection_id": "BC-1f624107c80ea1aecdac", "ref": "WLC:Ps.69.22", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gall for food and vinegar for thirst", "note": "The commentary's food obstruction, thirst and bitter remedy converge in one sufferer's plea; the psalm says enemies give gall for food and vinegar for thirst, turning what should nourish and refresh into affliction. This is WLC verse 22."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b36dc8010f83ad0465a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.75.9", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "The cup of divine wrath and its dregs", "note": "The psalm's cup contains foaming wine and dregs that the wicked must drink; it turns contained liquid toward judgment rather than the commentary's medicinal extract."}
{"connection_id": "BC-373d7ea308089c170f7c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.5", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س / Hebrew אנש, table sound correspondence with BDB cognate note", "note": "The commentary puts al-insan under pressure and loss; the psalm asks what frail man is that God remembers him, using אנוש for humanity whose smallness receives unexpected divine attention. This is WLC verse 5 after the superscription."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ddcaffda75d891c7429e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.6", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "frail human crowned with honor", "note": "The commentary's al-insan is liable to diminution; after asking what frail humanity is, the psalm says God crowns the human with glory and honor, continuing the human scene with elevation rather than loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-061ded8849f25fbb7cc8", "ref": "WLC:Song.4.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Locked garden; sealed spring", "note": "The commentary's stopper keeps the bitter extract inside its vessel; the poem's sealed spring likewise contains a source, but presents closure as erotic exclusivity rather than medicine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bed8d3892c9352f92fe2", "ref": "WLC:Song.4.12", "target": "sec3", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sealed fountain", "note": "The commentary's stopper and vessel preserve a valuable liquid; the beloved is called an enclosed garden and sealed fountain, so closure marks the guarded worth of what can flow from within."}
